go-tuiMaps draws that line on the map by itself. An application should also
give the credit in full, with the links above, in its About.

What was changed. Zoom 0 to 3 of the planet file named in PIN was decoded and
written again with only what go-tuiMaps reads: the layers it draws, each
feature's class and rank, and its name in the local language and in English.
Nothing was added, moved or corrected. tools/gen-assets is the program that
did it, and running it again on the same planet file writes the same bytes.
The tiles are offered under the same Open Database License as the data they
were made from, and the planet file they were cut from is offered to anyone
by its publisher.

What the hash list proves. HASHES holds the SHA-256 of every tile as it was
read and as it was written, and names the PIN it was made for. It shows
that the tiles here are unchanged since they were generated,
not that the source was authentic: the planet file was taken from its publisher over a secure
connection and was not checked against anything else.
